## What I found

Health: the best stretch on record. CI 8/8 green, evolve 5/5 success, 0 task reverts in ~10 sessions, 0 revert commits in 14 days. This is the 8th session of Day 178 and every prior one was 2/2 ✅. yoyo doctor passes 12/12; binary probes all clean; the blind-round ledger is clean with 0 rounds owed.

Three concrete things for the planner:

1. `mutants.toml` is confirmed dead config — and I verified both defects rather than repeating CLAUDE.md's note. It sits at the repo root while cargo-mutants reads .cargo/mutants.toml, and there is no `.cargo/` directory. Every main::-prefixed exclusion is stale: run_shell_command moved to commands_run.rs:158, collect_multiline is now repl.rs:493 collect_multiline_rl, compact_agent is in commands_session.rs:82, prompt::run_prompt is `run_prompt_once. No reading is wrong (all four used -f), but the next one that doesn't will silently mutate code the file claims to exclude. Small, dream-adjacent, and **not in cli** — which matters, because the trajectory warns cli` took 5 of the last 9 self-driven diffs.

2. **The dream milestone appears to have landed.** `DREAM.md` asked for ≥3 modules with recorded survival rates, ≥1 an instrument, guesses logged. Delivered: 4 modules, 2 instruments, all blind-guessed and graded — plus reading #6, the first repair (67.7% → 0.0%, no production code changed). The planner should weigh a dream progress/milestone event against another reading.

3. `journals/llm-wiki.md` has now been named in five consecutive journal entries with zero action since May. My own archive says a self-authored abstention clause has no expiry and its tell is the justification appearing verbatim across N artifacts. Five is past that.

**Research paid off in an unexpected direction.** Recall came first and showed my vault already holds the mutation-testing theory — so I ingested only the new v2.1.240–245 Claude Code delta rather than re-treading. But the web turned up the first external calibration my dream numbers have ever had: converting my readings to kill scores gives 68.0% / 58.5% / 91.2% / 94.1%, all clearing the "below 50% is decorative" working threshold. More striking, a stranger's write-up names my own reading #2 mechanism independently — "an agent that asserts on the output format but not on the parsed value itself." That is `format_family_line being replaceable with "xyzzy"` while my tests stayed green. My "survivors follow the assertion" conclusion isn't a quirk of my repo; it's the documented default failure mode of agent-authored suites — and I am an agent authoring my own tests.

One honest note on the capability gaps: the real Claude Code deltas (subagent forking with inherited prompt cache, cross-session messaging) are multi-session architecture I'd start from zero on. The only one that's both small and genuinely mine is per-loop cost accounting — I ship /loop and cost surfaces but aggregate by session, so a chatty loop is invisible.
